<%# The Money hub — the reference implementation of the six-block anatomy in
    docs/ABOUT_PLAN.md §2. Blocks 3 (tools) and 5 (features) are rendered by
    hub.html.erb around this partial; what lives here is 1, 2, 4 and 6. %>

<section class="mt-12 max-w-3xl space-y-5">
  <p>
    Most people are not bad with money. They are working from an incomplete
    picture: the subscription that renewed at a price nobody agreed to, the
    benefit they qualified for and never claimed, the debt whose interest
    they have never actually seen written down. The advice industry answers
    this with discipline. The real answer is usually information.
  </p>
  <p>
    This is the part of the app that holds the picture — without connecting
    to your bank, because handing a bank login to any software is a bad trade
    and it turns out not to be necessary.
  </p>
</section>

<section class="mt-14">
  <h2 class="display text-2xl">What you see</h2>
  <div class="mt-6 grid grid-cols-1 gap-4 sm:grid-cols-2">
    <%= render Ui::CardComponent.new(title: "Where it went") do %>
      <p class="text-sm text-soft">
        A ledger you fill by importing the statement your bank already gives
        you, or by logging a spend in about four seconds. Once a purchase has
        been sorted somewhere, the same purchase sorts itself next month.
      </p>
    <% end %>
    <%= render Ui::CardComponent.new(title: "What's coming") do %>
      <p class="text-sm text-soft">
        Bills and renewals as real dates on the same calendar as everything
        else in your life — so the car insurance turns up next to the school
        holiday, which is when it is actually useful to know.
      </p>
    <% end %>
    <%= render Ui::CardComponent.new(title: "What you owe") do %>
      <p class="text-sm text-soft">
        Every debt in one list, with the interest written down, and an order
        to pay them off in that you can see the reasoning for.
      </p>
    <% end %>
    <%= render Ui::CardComponent.new(title: "What you're paying for monthly") do %>
      <p class="text-sm text-soft">
        Subscriptions, gathered up — including the ones that arrived as a
        free trial three years ago. Cancelling one is a tool, because the
        hard part was never the decision.
      </p>
    <% end %>
  </div>
</section>

<section class="mt-14 max-w-3xl">
  <h2 class="display text-2xl">What it does without being asked</h2>
  <p class="mt-4 text-soft">
    As much or as little as you want. Each area of the app has a dial: leave
    it at the bottom and it never speaks unless spoken to; turn it up and it
    will tell you that a renewal is coming, that this month looks unlike the
    last three, or that something you're entitled to has opened. You set the
    dial per area, and you can move it back down.
  </p>
</section>

<section class="mt-14 max-w-3xl">
  <h2 class="display text-2xl">Who can see it</h2>
  <p class="mt-4 text-soft">
    Nobody, until you say so. Money is the area where that matters most and
    where households differ most — some share everything, some share the
    bills and not the salaries, some share nothing. The app takes no view.
    You can share a single item or the whole area with another person in the
    house, and see at a glance what you've shared.
  </p>
</section>
